<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="kaya">

  <!-- ==================================================================
       REGENERATED FROM CAD 2026-08-29 (Fusion 360 parametric model).
       Source of truth: cad/kaya_dimensions.yaml

       MEASURED and trustworthy:
         wheel geometry, contact radius, wheel angles, camera pose,
         total mass, servo + camera envelopes (vendor STEP), and the
         shell profile (35 mm edges, 60 mm ridge, 25 mm ledge at 5 deg).

       DERIVED from measurements (no longer guessed):
         upper deck height. The camera sits on the 60 mm shell ridge and
         its centre is measured at 165 mm above the floor, so the deck top
         is 165 - 29.1/2 - 60 = 90.45 mm. The model then puts the shell
         ledge at 150.45 mm and the camera underside at 148.26 mm - they
         meet within 0.2 mm, which is an independent cross-check of the
         165 mm, the 4.89 deg IMU tilt and the 60 mm/5 deg shell.

       INTERIOR, from the deck photo: Jetson mounted with its PORTS AFT
       and its own heatsink fan forward, sitting under the front vent
       panel - so no separate chassis fan exists. Buck converters,
       terminal block and power switch modelled as envelopes at the rear
       apex. All four sit on the deck but outside the shell footprint,
       which is consistent: the shell's long sides are open and the buck
       converter is visible through that gap in the assembled photos.

       ALSO MEASURED 2026-08-29: shell sheet 2 mm everywhere, top vent
       patterns, sides open along both long edges. Jetson envelope taken
       from the wendy.dev OBJ mesh: 90.74 (fore-aft, ports REARWARD)
       x 103.0 (left-right) x 34.77 mm tall. It FITS, with about 7 mm of
       headroom at its front edge - but only just, and only in the window
       x = 4.6 to 17.6 mm. Note the CAD models it as a solid ABS envelope
       at 344 g against a real devkit's ~250 g.

       STILL ESTIMATED:
         PLATE THICKNESS is now the dominant one. At the assumed 3 mm the
         two decks are 521 g and the robot totals +11.6% against the
         scale (after correcting the servo and Jetson proxies). At 2 mm
         they are 347 g and the total falls to about +3%. Calipers on the
         plate edge is the next measurement worth taking.
         Also: middle deck height,
         standoff placement, battery and Jetson volumes, coupler and
         bracket sizes. base_link inertia below is computed from real
         geometry, but that geometry rests on ~18 estimated numbers.
       ================================================================== -->

  <xacro:property name="wheel_radius"   value="0.037"/>   <!-- MEASURED -->
  <xacro:property name="wheel_width"    value="0.025"/>   <!-- MEASURED -->
  <!-- This is L in the omni kinematics (v_i = L*w), which is not quite the
       same thing as the CAD's geometric contact_radius (143 mm): L is
       whatever value makes odometry's yaw rate match the chassis' TRUE
       physical yaw rate, and folds in small compression/slip effects the
       geometric measurement can't see. Calibrated 2026-09-03 by spinning
       the robot ~10 turns and comparing odometry's integrated yaw to a
       visually-counted true rotation - see kaya_dimensions.yaml
       wheels.kinematic_L_mm for the full derivation. Single rough-precision
       trial; worth repeating with a precise angle marker before Phase 4. -->
  <xacro:property name="chassis_radius" value="0.138"/>   <!-- CALIBRATED 2026-09-03, was 0.143 -->

  <xacro:property name="deck_radius"      value="0.1195"/> <!-- CAD, outline ESTIMATED -->
  <xacro:property name="footprint_radius" value="0.160"/>  <!-- DERIVED, encloses the wheels -->

  <!-- Chassis envelope from CAD: retainer underside 20 mm above the floor
       to shell ridge 150.45 mm. 130 mm tall, centred 85 mm up, so 48 mm
       above base_link. -->
  <xacro:property name="chassis_height" value="0.130"/>
  <xacro:property name="chassis_z"      value="0.048"/>

  <xacro:property name="camera_x" value="0.02181"/>       <!-- DERIVED from the shell -->
  <xacro:property name="camera_y" value="0.000"/>         <!-- MEASURED -->
  <xacro:property name="camera_z" value="${0.17372 - wheel_radius}"/>  <!-- DERIVED, see note -->
  <xacro:property name="camera_pitch" value="-0.0853"/>   <!-- MEASURED (IMU), nose UP -->
  <!-- ROLL SIGN CORRECTED 2026-09-27: was -0.0164. The magnitude came from
       the IMU but the sign flipped converting to REP-103. At rest the real
       Gemini IMU reads accel x = -0.161 m/s^2 in its optical frame (x right),
       i.e. gravity pulls toward the camera's RIGHT: the right side is LOWER,
       which is POSITIVE roll about +X. Isaac Sim, with the Orbbec driver's own
       IMU placement, reproduced -0.161 only with this sign (+0.160 with the
       old one). Pitch was right: sim +0.835 vs real +0.834 on the z axis. -->
  <xacro:property name="camera_roll"  value="0.0164"/>    <!-- MEASURED (IMU) -->

  <!-- Gemini 335L from Orbbec's STEP. Robot axes: X = depth (optical),
       Y = the 95 mm stereo baseline, Z = height. -->
  <xacro:property name="camera_box_x" value="0.0293"/>
  <xacro:property name="camera_box_y" value="0.1241"/>
  <xacro:property name="camera_box_z" value="0.0291"/>

  <xacro:property name="total_mass" value="2.088"/>       <!-- MEASURED -->
  <!-- MEASURED 2026-09-27 on a scale: 112 g for one wheel (hub + rollers),
       WITHOUT the shaft coupler. The CAD said 58.45 g - it models the
       rollers as solid rubber, and the real ones carry metal pins/hubs. -->
  <xacro:property name="wheel_mass" value="0.112"/>       <!-- MEASURED -->

  <!-- base_link mass from CAD geometry + real densities.
       With the MEASURED wheel, CAD (base 1.5426 + 3 x 0.112 wheels + 0.164
       camera) = 2.043 kg vs 2.088 on the scale: 2.2% light. The old 6.7% gap
       was blamed on the servos' shape-only STEP; the wheels were most of it.
       The rest may still be the servos - weigh one to close it out. -->
  <!-- ##################################################################
       SHELL MATERIAL - RESOLVED. Not magnetic, so aluminium. This also
       matched what the mass budget predicted independently (see below).

       The shell is 2 mm sheet everywhere (MEASURED), described as
       "powder coated iron kind of material". At STEEL density that shell
       is 602 g and base_link alone came to 2.109 kg, heavier than the
       whole robot weighs on the scale.

       Testing the steel hypothesis properly: credit it the Jetson
       correction (solid ABS envelope 344 g -> a real devkit's ~250 g)
       AND halve the battery retainer, and it is still 223 g over. That
       has to come out of the decks, which would need to be 1.38 mm
       plate - before any allowance for wiring, fasteners, PCBs,
       terminal blocks, antennas or the switch. A 1.4 mm plate carrying
       three servos and a battery pack is not credible.

       The magnet test then confirmed it: NOT MAGNETIC. Aluminium, with
       powder coat hiding it by eye.

       DECK OUTLINE NOW MEASURED (2026-08-29): equiangular hexagon, three
       195 mm sides and three 50 mm corner flats. Parent triangle side
       195 + 2x50 = 295 mm, giving an edge apothem of 85.16 mm and a
       corner apothem of 127.02 mm. My earlier estimates (75 / 118) were
       both low, which is why the Jetson and the rear electronics would
       not previously fit.

       STRUCTURE, corrected 2026-08-29:
         The three servos hang from the UPPER (triangular) deck on long
         custom U brackets, 72 mm from the servo underside up to the deck.
         Each bracket is now ONE body: a saddle web sitting on the servo
         top face (67.10-70.10) with TWO arms rising to the deck. Verified
         flush against the case - the three shells run continuously,
         BOTTOMCASE 63.20-80.00, MIDDLECASE 80.00-96.70, TOPCABINET
         96.70-110.50, so the arms meet the case at both ends with no gap.
         The arms land on the servo's FLAT MOUNTING FACES (the ones with
         the four screw holes), not on the case bounding extremes. Those
         faces are inboard of the extremes by measured amounts: 5.702 mm
         on the inner side (the extreme there is the rear cover boss) and
         1.49 mm on the outer (the extreme there is the output boss).
         So the arms sit at radius 65.902-68.902 and 109.010-112.010, and
         the bracket touches the servo on both faces with zero gap and
         zero interference against any servo body.

         Geometrically: TWO arms per servo, on both faces perpendicular to
         the shaft (fore and aft along the servo axis), running from the
         servo MID-HEIGHT (47.00) up to the deck underside (98.00).
         Stopping at mid-height keeps them clear of the output shaft at
         37.00 by 10 mm - they clamp the body, never the axis.
         Bracket width is 20.20 mm, EXACTLY the servo body width. An
         earlier revision made it 26.20 (body + 3 mm each side); that
         overhang read as a gap in perspective views even though the arms
         were flush.
         All three plates and the base triangle carry 5 mm rounded corners,
         and every sheet bend is filleted (1.5 inner / 3.5 outer).

       SERVO SHAFT ALIGNMENT: the servos are now positioned by their OUTPUT
       SHAFT, placed exactly on the wheel axle at 37 mm - which is what
       matters for rotation. The consequence is that the vendor STEP puts
       the case underside at 26.90, against your measured 25.00. The 1.90 mm
       is internal to Waveshare's model (it has 10.1 mm from shaft to case
       bottom; your measurements imply 12.0). Shaft alignment wins.
         They do NOT attach to the middle plate. The brackets shipped in
         Waveshare's STEP are the short standard ones and have been
         deleted - this robot uses longer custom parts.
         The middle plate is NOT triangular: it is a 95 x 95 square, the
         same outline as the battery plate below it, and carries no load
         from the drive. Four standoffs upper->middle, four middle->bottom,
         in aligned columns at +/-43 mm.

       LOWER STACK MEASURED 2026-08-29 (floor upward):
         battery plate 30.00-32.00 (95x95 square, 43x74 slide slot open at
         the front), 35 mm standoffs, middle plate 67.00-69.00 (95x95
         square), 28 mm standoffs, upper deck 97.00-99.00.
       CROSS-CHECK: the servo case runs 25.00-65.20 and the upper deck
       underside is at 97.00, so the custom bracket spans 72 mm - which is
       exactly the "long U bracket" on the real robot. The measured servo
       standoff and the measured plate stack agree without adjustment.

       SHELL PROFILE, fully MEASURED 2026-08-29, above the base triangle
       (sheet thickness excluded from all of these):
         front edge 34, rises to 60 at 43 mm back from the front edge,
         then a 33 mm camera ledge dropping to 55, then down to 30 at the
         rear edge. Camera front face overhangs 12 mm FORWARD of the ledge.
       The shell is very nearly flush with the base plate: only 2 mm inside
       at the FRONT and 4 mm inside at the REAR (both MEASURED), so the
       outline is derived from the plate rather than a uniform inset.
       The measured 34/60/55/30 heights are to the sheet UNDERSIDE, so the
       outer face is 2 mm above each. That makes the ledge outer face at
       the rear bend 100+55+2 = 157.00 mm - matching the measured 157
       exactly, which confirms the reading convention.
       The camera REAR face rests on that rear bend, so camera_x is half a
       camera depth forward of it: 21.81 mm. (The alternative anchor -
       front face 12 mm behind the 60 mm bend - is inconsistent by 8.3 mm,
       because 12 + 29.3 needs a 41.3 mm ledge and it is only 33.)
       All sheet bends are rounded, not sharp: inner radius 1.5 mm, outer
       3.5 mm (= inner + thickness), as a folded sheet actually behaves.

       LEDGE ANGLE: 60->55 over 33 mm is 8.62 deg, but the IMU measures the
       camera at 4.89 deg. A 2.2 mm reading error over 33 mm covers that,
       and the IMU is far more precise, so the camera pose keeps 4.89.

       *** THE 8.55 mm CONFLICT IS RESOLVED ***
       Floor to the top of the base triangle MEASURED at 100.00 mm. The
       lower plate chain (30+2+35+2+28+2) independently predicts 99.00 -
       agreement to 1 mm. The early direct camera reading of 165 mm was
       simply wrong and is discarded. upper_deck_top_above_floor is now
       the measured 100.00, and every height above it follows from the
       measured shell profile.

       2026-08-29, further MEASURED: plate thickness 2 mm (same stock as the
       shell), servo underside 25 mm above the floor with NO bottom bracket
       (they hang from long CUSTOM brackets off the UPPER deck; both
       Waveshare stock brackets, FK-US-001 and FK-US-002, were deleted as
       they do not match this build), and the
       shell is closed at the FRONT and the REAR, and fully open along both
       long sides - no side walls at all.
       MPU-6050 removed, not in use.

       CROSS-CHECK: the corner flat lands at radius 127.0 mm and the wheel
       assembly inner face at 130.5 mm - a 3.5 mm running clearance, which
       falls out of three independently measured numbers (contact radius
       143, wheel width 25, plate 195/50).
       ################################################################## -->

  <!-- base_link mass is SCALED so the four rigid bodies sum to the measured
       2.088 kg. The CAD geometry gives 1.5426 kg; multiplying by 1.0294 gets
       the total right while KEEPING the CAD's mass DISTRIBUTION, which is
       what the inertia tensor actually encodes. The inertia below is scaled
       by the same factor. This is why you do NOT need to weigh individual
       parts: one total plus the CAD distribution is enough for PhysX.
       2026-09-27: factor was 1.1336 (base 1.74864) until the wheel was
       weighed; the inertia below was rescaled by 1.588/1.74864 with it.
       The 10.8 g shaft coupler (CAD) is counted here in base_link. -->
  <xacro:property name="base_mass"  value="1.588"/>       <!-- = 2.088 - 3*wheel - camera -->
  <xacro:property name="base_com_z" value="0.05225"/>     <!-- 89.3 mm above the floor -->

  <!-- base_link inertia about its own COM, base_link axes.
       ixz is genuinely non-zero: the shell slopes down toward the rear, so
       the chassis is not fore-aft symmetric. ixy and iyz are ~1e-18 and
       dropped. The old solid-cylinder placeholder overstated izz by 3.5x,
       which for a holonomic base is the term that dominates yaw response. -->
  <xacro:property name="base_ixx" value="5.7879e-03"/>
  <xacro:property name="base_iyy" value="6.3183e-03"/>
  <xacro:property name="base_izz" value="7.4579e-03"/>
  <xacro:property name="base_ixz" value="-1.7872e-04"/>

  <!-- CAD distribution, scaled by 112/58.45 to the measured wheel mass -->
  <xacro:property name="wheel_ixx" value="7.1080e-05"/>   <!-- polar, spin axis -->
  <xacro:property name="wheel_iyy" value="4.3294e-05"/>   <!-- transverse -->
  <xacro:property name="wheel_izz" value="4.3294e-05"/>   <!-- transverse -->

  <material name="grey">  <color rgba="0.28 0.29 0.31 1.0"/></material>
  <material name="black"> <color rgba="0.10 0.10 0.12 1.0"/></material>
  <material name="teal">  <color rgba="0.05 0.55 0.62 1.0"/></material>

  <link name="base_footprint"/>

  <link name="base_link">
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 ${chassis_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><cylinder radius="${deck_radius}" length="${chassis_height}"/></geometry>
      <material name="grey"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0 0 ${chassis_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><cylinder radius="${deck_radius}" length="${chassis_height}"/></geometry>
    </collision>
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0 0 ${base_com_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="${base_mass}"/>
      <inertia ixx="${base_ixx}" iyy="${base_iyy}" izz="${base_izz}"
               ixy="0" ixz="${base_ixz}" iyz="0"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>

  <joint name="base_footprint_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_footprint"/><child link="base_link"/>
    <origin xyz="0 0 ${wheel_radius}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- TWO WHEELS FORWARD flanking the camera, ONE AT THE REAR: 60/180/300.

       SERVO ID -> ANGLE: RESOLVED 2026-09-03 by pulsing each servo
       individually in wheel mode (low speed, ~35 deg, robot on the floor)
       and watching which physical wheel turned and which way:
         servo 1 -> front-right -> theta = 300 deg (top moved toward front)
         servo 2 -> front-left  -> theta =  60 deg (top moved toward back)
         servo 3 -> rear        -> theta = 180 deg (top moved toward robot's right)
       Every servo's positive Goal Speed matched the standard kinematic sign
       convention (v_i = -sin(th_i)*vx + cos(th_i)*vy + L*w) directly - no
       per-wheel flip needed. See kaya_dimensions.yaml servo_wheel_map and
       ~/wheel_probe.py on the robot.

       CAD JOINT SIGN caveat (see the old warning this replaces): the note
       about Fusion's as-built joints spinning anti-parallel to axis="1 0 0"
       is about the CAD's OWN joint definitions if this file is ever
       regenerated straight from CAD joint transforms. It does not apply to
       the hand-verified wheel_N -> theta assignment below, which is the
       real, tested value - axis="1 0 0" with these thetas is what actually
       drove the robot correctly (forward, rotate, and strafe all verified
       against odometry and visual observation, 2026-09-03). If a future
       regeneration flips this, re-verify with wheel_probe.py rather than
       trusting the CAD sign blindly.

       The CAD now carries 3 wheel joints plus 36 roller joints (12 per
       wheel, each about its own tangential axis). The rollers are there
       for visual and kinematic sanity only - the URDF and Isaac still
       simulate each wheel as ONE cylinder with anisotropic friction. -->
  <!-- MESHES: exported from Fusion in the BASE_LINK frame, so a wheel mesh
       carries its assembled position. The compensating visual origin below
       undoes the joint transform exactly:
           xyz = -chassis_radius on local X,  rpy = 0 0 -theta
       Swap the cylinder for the mesh line when you wire up Isaac.
       NOTE: wheel_N's theta below was reassigned 2026-09-03 (see the servo
       mapping note above) to match the real servo IDs. The cylinder used
       today is rotationally symmetric so this is a no-op visually, but if
       wheel_N.obj was exported from a Fusion component that assumed the OLD
       theta, its baked-in roller orientation may no longer match - purely
       cosmetic (collision/kinematics stay correct either way), but check
       before wiring the mesh line up for Isaac Sim. -->
  <xacro:macro name="omni_wheel" params="name theta">
    <link name="${name}_wheel_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 ${pi/2} 0"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="${wheel_radius}" length="${wheel_width}"/></geometry>
        <!-- mesh alternative:
        <origin xyz="${-chassis_radius} 0 0" rpy="0 0 ${-theta}"/>
        <geometry><mesh filename="package://kaya_description/meshes/${name}.obj"/></geometry>
        -->
        <material name="black"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <!-- Keep this a CYLINDER even after the roller mesh is exported.
             Omni behaviour comes from anisotropic friction, not from
             simulating twelve free rollers. -->
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 ${pi/2} 0"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="${wheel_radius}" length="${wheel_width}"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <mass value="${wheel_mass}"/>
        <!-- FIXED: the pre-CAD revision put the polar moment on iyy while
             declaring the spin axis as X. -->
        <inertia ixx="${wheel_ixx}" iyy="${wheel_iyy}" izz="${wheel_izz}"
                 ixy="0" ixz="0" iyz="0"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${name}_wheel_joint" type="continuous">
      <parent link="base_link"/><child link="${name}_wheel_link"/>
      <origin xyz="${chassis_radius*cos(theta)} ${chassis_radius*sin(theta)} 0"
              rpy="0 0 ${theta}"/>
      <axis xyz="1 0 0"/>
    </joint>
  </xacro:macro>

  <xacro:omni_wheel name="wheel_1" theta="${5*pi/3}"/>    <!-- 300 deg, front-right, servo id 1 -->
  <xacro:omni_wheel name="wheel_2" theta="${pi/3}"/>      <!-- 60 deg, front-left, servo id 2 -->
  <xacro:omni_wheel name="wheel_3" theta="${pi}"/>        <!-- 180 deg, rear, servo id 3 -->

  <!-- We publish base_link -> camera_link ONLY; the Orbbec driver supplies
       the rest of the chain on /tf_static. The 164 g camera has no inertial
       block, so its mass is carried implicitly by base_link.

       camera_link IS THE LEFT IR LENS, not the camera body centre.
       The driver's /tf_static has camera_link -> camera_depth_frame as the
       identity, and depth = the left IR camera; so camera_link must be where
       the left IR lens physically is. Until 2026-09-27 this joint put
       camera_link at the BODY centre (camera_x/y/z above), which placed every
       depth point ~47.5 mm to the right of the truth.

       MEASURED on the robot 2026-09-27: a bottle on the centre line at 0.5 m
       and 1.0 m, read from depth. With the old joint it sat at -44.5 and
       -38.9 mm (fit: -51.0 mm offset, +0.65 deg yaw - an offset, not a yaw);
       with this joint at +3.0 and +8.6 mm, i.e. on the line to within
       hand placement.

       The lens position is DERIVED from the measured body centre: the lens
       is 2.3 mm forward, 47.5 mm left and 0.55 mm up of the body centre in
       the camera's own axes (Orbbec's geometry, via NVIDIA's Gemini 335L
       asset: 95 mm baseline, lens 11.2 mm behind the cover glass), rotated
       by the mount rpy below. The box is drawn back at the body centre. -->
  <xacro:property name="camera_link_x" value="0.02399"/>
  <xacro:property name="camera_link_y" value="0.04748"/>
  <xacro:property name="camera_link_z" value="${0.17524 - wheel_radius}"/>
  <link name="camera_link">
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="-0.0023 -0.0475 -0.00055" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="${camera_box_x} ${camera_box_y} ${camera_box_z}"/></geometry>
      <material name="teal"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="-0.0023 -0.0475 -0.00055" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="${camera_box_x} ${camera_box_y} ${camera_box_z}"/></geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>
  <joint name="camera_mount_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link"/><child link="camera_link"/>
    <origin xyz="${camera_link_x} ${camera_link_y} ${camera_link_z}"
            rpy="${camera_roll} ${camera_pitch} 0"/>
  </joint>


</robot>
